{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And Yitzhak was forty years old, etc.</b> The reason it was necessary to inform us of the time of his marriage — something not done with Avraham — is to give the reason for the delay, that Yitzhak remained unmarried until forty years had passed. And it states the reason: <b>when he took Rivkah</b> — for the taking of Rivkah he waited until forty years, as its conclusion says <b>to him for a wife</b>, meaning: uniquely designated for him, she is his destined mate. From this you learn that before her he had no mate, as we have written in the previous parashah. And she was young, having been born at the Akedah when he was rendered fit to procreate. From this there is no grievance against Yitzhak for waiting until forty years, nor against Avraham for delaying until forty years, for at that time she was three years old according to the reckoning." "<b>Daughter of Betuel the Aramean, etc., sister, etc.</b> The reason the verse repeats to inform us of this is not to tell us whose daughter she is, but to teach a defense on Rivkah's behalf. For it says nearby (Genesis 25:21): ״וַיֵּעָתֶר לוֹ ה׳״ — to him and not to her, and reason would lead one to say that the reason for this is because of Rivkah's deficiency, Heaven forbid, for she is not worthy that Elohim listen to her. Therefore it prefaced by saying <b>daughter of Betuel</b>, to say that this is the reason why HaShem responded to Yitzhak — for he is the son of Avraham and she is the daughter of Betuel. And it repeated saying <b>the Aramean of Padan Aram</b> to hint that in saying ״the Aramean״ the intention is to say ״the deceiver״ (ha-ramai), for if the intention were to identify him by the name of his city, that has already been stated. And the intent in this is that since his wickedness was not publicly known in the world, it comes to inform us that he was wicked, and through his mediation Rivkah's prayer was not as accepted as Yitzhak's prayer." "And its saying <b>sister of Lavan</b> appears unnecessary, in addition to the fact that we already know it. Rather, the reason is that it comes to teach merit on Rivkah's behalf, by showing us that she gave birth to a manifestly wicked son, and one might ask: how from a blessed womb came forth one who is cursed — therefore it says that this is the reason for the matter: that she was the sister of Lavan the Aramean, meaning the deceiver, with wickedness added upon that of his father. And our Sages of blessed memory (Bava Batra 110a) already preceded [me], [speaking of] the majority of sons, for the known reason, and therefore Esav emerged; but for this, her offspring would all have been righteous, for she was perfectly righteous, and the proof is ״facing his wife״, and our Sages of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 63) etc. And lest you say: if so, why did Yitzhak not pay attention to investigate her brother — therefore it concludes by saying <b>to him for a wife</b>, as I explained above; and besides this, he would not have married the sister of a wicked man." "<b>Further</b>, there is a hint in the informing of <b>sister of Lavan</b>, following what they of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 63:13) — that the reason Rivkah was barren is because of Lavan's blessing, so that it not appear that his blessing accomplishes anything good. Therefore, before the verse states that she was barren, it prefaced by stating the reason for the matter and said ״sister of Lavan״ — and this was the reason Rivkah was barren, until they needed prayer."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:25.283512875Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}